Sval: a received shared memory handle must be as large as it claims

Backport of Mozilla's Bug 2045119 (mozilla-central 0112087d1a12 and its
gtests 1485e9ce0da0, Firefox 155), unchanged. ESR 153 does not carry it.
A shared memory handle read from IPC was mapped at the size the sender
claimed. On Linux a child could shrink the memfd behind a handle it had
sent, as with the idle scheduler's counter, and the parent crashed with
SIGBUS on its next read. Platform::IsSafeToMap takes the claimed size:
on Linux a memfd is sealed against shrinking and growing where the
kernel allows it, and the reader checks the seal, and the size, before
mapping; Android checks the size, and macOS and Windows leave the check
to the kernel. The Linux sandbox lets every process read seals and add
those two. TestSharedMemory.cpp's new cases are Mozilla's gtests for it.
Drop this patch when the upstream pin reaches Firefox 155.

